A plan that satisfies every invariant: `check` is clean, and the other
commands answer from it.

`A-001` is `done` and cites a commit, and this run never opened git to look at
it -- so the clean answer comes with the note that says which half of the gate
just ran. It is here, in the canonical clean plan, because that is exactly where
"ok" is most likely to be read as more than it is.

```
$ aplan check
ok: 2 tasks, 0 warning(s), DAG acyclic
note: `check` is structural and did not open the repository, so 1 citation(s) on 1 task(s) are unchecked rather than passing -- a cited commit or test that has rotted passes here. `aplan verify` is the half that resolves them; run it in the same gate.

```

```
$ aplan ready
A-002  Ship the validator (3d)

```

```
$ aplan why A-002
A-002 is ready: nothing blocks it

```

```
$ aplan graph
graph TD
    A-001(["A-001: Ship the loader"])
    A-002["A-002: Ship the validator"]
    A-001 --> A-002

```

The schema is derived from the Rust types, so pin only its shape here; drift
is caught by `cargo xtask gen-schema --check`, not by a snapshot.

```
$ aplan schema
{
  "$defs": {
...
  "required": [
    "id",
    "title",
    "phase",
    "status"
  ],
  "title": "Task",
  "type": "object"
}

```

`aplan facts` prints the tool's generated facts as JSON. Its contents (the rule,
format, and command lists, and their counts) are pinned by `tests/facts.rs`
against `facts.json`; here we only confirm the command runs and emits an object,
so this case never needs updating when a rule is added.

```
$ aplan facts
{
  "behaviours": [
...
}

```
